--TEST--
Every token Core\Jwt::sign writes is three non-empty base64url segments with no padding and no `+` or `/`, over a sweep of keys and claim widths, and the URL alphabet is not hypothetical — ADR 0060 §§ 1, 4
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Invariance over a sweep, counted. A JWT is carried in a URL, a header and a
// cookie, so the encoding is part of the contract and not an implementation
// detail — but a single token is nearly silent about it: whether `+`, `/` or
// `=` ever appears depends on the bytes that happened to be signed and on the
// length that happened to result. So the question is asked of twelve tokens
// under two keys and six claim widths, which is what moves the payload's
// length through every residue that padding would show up at.
secret bytes $first = Core\Crypto::generateKey();
secret bytes $second = Core\Crypto::generateKey();

array<int> $widths = [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
array<string> $tokens = [];
foreach ($widths as int $n) {
    string $pad = Core\Str::repeat("a", $n as uint);
    $tokens[] = Core\Jwt::sign(["sub" => "ada" . $pad], 15m, $first);
    $tokens[] = Core\Jwt::sign(["sub" => "ada" . $pad, "role" => $pad], 1h, $second);
}

int $threeParts = 0;
int $segments = 0;
int $clean = 0;
int $renamed = 0;
foreach ($tokens as string $token) {
    array<string> $parts = Core\Str::split($token, ".");
    if ((Core\Arr::count($parts) as int) == 3) {
        $threeParts = $threeParts + 1;
    }
    foreach ($parts as string $part) {
        $segments = $segments + 1;
        // Non-empty is part of the same property: a `sign` that wrote an empty
        // signature would still split into three.
        if (!Core\Str::isEmpty($part)
            && !Core\Str::contains($part, "=")
            && !Core\Str::contains($part, "+")
            && !Core\Str::contains($part, "/")) {
            $clean = $clean + 1;
        }
        if (Core\Str::contains($part, "-") || Core\Str::contains($part, "_")) {
            $renamed = $renamed + 1;
        }
    }
}

echo Core\Arr::count($tokens), " tokens, ", $threeParts, " of them three segments", "\n";
echo $clean, " of ", $segments, " segments are non-empty, unpadded and url-safe", "\n";

// And the sweep is not vacuous. Every segment above could be plain base64 and
// pass the counts if no signature happened to contain one of the two bytes the
// URL alphabet renames; twelve random 32-byte tags make that outcome
// vanishingly unlikely, so this line is what says the property was tested
// rather than merely not contradicted.
if ($renamed > 0) {
    echo "and at least one segment carries a byte standard base64 spells `+` or `/`", "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
12 tokens, 12 of them three segments
36 of 36 segments are non-empty, unpadded and url-safe
and at least one segment carries a byte standard base64 spells `+` or `/`
